content.text:
But catch-up for old save age 12 with stage baby->null: p.age>=5 && <11 false, so no elementary; no middle. Need robust range: if !stage and !droppedOut and age >=5, choose based on age:
```
if (!e.stage && !e.droppedOut && p.age>=5) {
 if age<11 elementary
 else if age<14 middle
 else if age<18 high
 else { e.level="hs"? } 
}
```
Better. Let's refactor to handle all at once. Current code first condition requires age<11, so age 12 no start. Let's modify to a catch-up block.

Replace initial block with:
```
if (!e.stage && !e.droppedOut && p.age >= 5) {
  if (p.age < 11) { elementary... }
  else if (p.age < 14) { middle; e.level? none }
  else if (p.age < 18) { high }
  else { e.level = "hs"; addNote graduated? }
}
```
But for age >=18 and no stage, should they be considered high school graduates? If old save never went to school, we could set level hs and note. But new profiles age 18 have stage high then graduation block. For a fresh profile aged to 18 with fix, stage will be high so this block false and graduation block runs. Good. For old save age 30 stage null, set level hs? That seems okay as catch-up.

However, there's another concern: at age 18 if stage high, our first block `!e.stage` false. graduation block runs. Good.

Let's simplify:
```
if (!e.stage && !e.droppedOut && p.age >=5) {
  if (p.age <11) ...
  else if (p.age <14) ...
  else if (p.age <18) ...
  else { e.level="hs"; e.gpa=...; addNote "I finished high school."; }
}
```
But existing next blocks `if (p.age >=11 && p.age<14 && e.stage === "elementary")` etc. If we just started elementary at age 6 in this same call, next block false (age<11). Fine. If catch-up age 12 starts middle, subsequent middle->high false. Good. If age 15 starts high, graduation false. Good.

Let's replace first block.

